tel 1.0

base contextual
import soundness

info   the machinery of checked string interpolators: literals parsed as the code compiles, holes typed by typeclass
detail
    _Contextual_ is the one piece of machinery behind every checked literal in Soundness — `url"…"`,
    `sh"…"`, `json"…"`, `t"…"`, `media"…"` — and the way to define new ones. An interpolator
    defined with it validates its literal parts as the code compiles, reports a fault at the
    exact character which caused it, and accepts substitutions only of the types it declares,
    each checked for the hole it fills. No hand-written macro is needed for the plumbing:
    capturing the literal parts and their positions in the source file is done once, by the
    `interpolation` macro, and an interpolator's author writes typeclass instances.

    Three pieces make an interpolator: a prefix method on `StringContext` which calls
    `interpolation[Target](context)` and returns its `Interpolation`; an `Interpolable` instance
    for the target type, which builds the value from the parts and the substituted values; and
    `Insertion` or `Substitution` instances, which say which types may fill a hole and how
    each enters. An `Extrapolable` instance makes the same literal usable as a pattern.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import scala.compiletime

    case class Slug(text: Text)

    object Slug:
      given interpolable: Slug is Interpolable:
        transparent inline def interpolate[parts <: Tuple, origins <: Tuple]
          ( inline insertions: Any* )
        :   Slug =
          Slug(compiletime.constValueTuple[parts].toList.reverse.mkString.tt)

    extension (inline context: StringContext)
      transparent inline def slug: Interpolation = interpolation[Slug](context)

    slug"hello"   // Slug(t"hello")
    ```

    The literal's parts reach `Interpolable#interpolate` as the type parameter `parts`, a tuple
    of string-literal types, and their source positions as `origins`, so a macro behind the
    instance can parse them and `halt` at `Interpolation.sourcePosition`. Because the prefix
    method is `transparent inline`, an interpolator may return a type more precise than its
    target: a path on a particular platform, a name proved valid, a document typed by its
    schema. The convention throughout Soundness is to pair every interpolator with a runtime
    parser running the same code (`url"…"` beside `as[Url]`), so a literal and dynamic text
    cannot drift apart.

    The library has a single source set, `contextual.core`, compiling for every platform, and
    everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella. It has no choice package: its givens
    live in companions and resolve through implicit scope.

refer contextual.Interpolation
refer contextual.interpolation
refer contextual.Interpolable
refer contextual.Extrapolable
refer contextual.Insertion
refer contextual.Substitution

entry .interpolation   the macro behind every interpolator's prefix method: captures a literal's parts and positions
    `interpolation[Target](context)` takes the `StringContext` of an interpolated literal, which
    must be a literal known to the compiler, and returns an `Interpolation` whose `Topic` is
    `Target` and whose `Transport` and `Origins` type members encode the literal's parts and
    their source positions. A prefix method is written as a `transparent inline` extension on an
    `inline` `StringContext`, so that the literal survives to the macro:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    extension (inline context: StringContext)
      transparent inline def media: Interpolation = interpolation[MediaType](context)
    ```

    `media"text/plain"` then calls `Interpolation#apply`, which hands the encoded parts to the
    `MediaType is Interpolable` instance; `case media"text/plain" =>` calls
    `Interpolation#unapply`, which hands them to the `MediaType is Extrapolable` instance.

entry .Interpolation   one interpolated literal, with its target, its parts and their positions as types
  detail
      An `Interpolation` is what a prefix method returns: a pure value carrying, as type members,
      the interpolator's target (`#Topic`), the literal parts (`#Transport`) and their source
      positions (`#Origins`). Calling it (`#apply`) constructs a value of the target type; using
      it as a pattern (`#unapply`) deconstructs one. Values are made only by the `interpolation`
      macro.

      Both type tuples are built innermost-first, so they hold the parts **in reverse**: for
      `slug"a${x}b${y}c"`, `constValueTuple[parts].toList` is `List("c", "b", "a")`. Every part
      is present, including empty ones, so a literal with `n` holes always has `n + 1` parts:
      `slug"$x"` has two empty parts.

      The companion holds the helpers an interpolator's own macro uses to turn the type-level
      parts and origins back into values and to report a fault at the right character.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import scala.quoted.*

      def check[parts <: Tuple: Type, origins <: Tuple: Type](using Quotes): Expr[Unit] =
        val parts: List[String] = Interpolation.decodeParts[parts]
        val origins: List[(Int, Int)] = Interpolation.decodeOrigins[origins]

        if parts.exists(_.contains("!"))
        then halt(m"`!` is not allowed", Interpolation.sourcePosition(parts, origins, 0, 0))
        else '{()}
      ```
  refer contextual.interpolation
  refer contextual.Interpolable
  entry .apply   the macro implementation of `interpolation`: builds the `Interpolation` for a literal
      Not called directly: `interpolation[topic](context)` splices it. It reads the parts of the
      `StringContext`, finds each part's start and end in the source file, and encodes both as
      tuple types on a new `Interpolation`.
  entry .decodeParts   the literal parts a `Transport` type encodes, in source order, inside a macro
      `Interpolation.decodeParts[parts]` needs a `Quotes` and a `Type[parts]`, and reverses the
      type-level tuple back into source order. A tuple element which is not a string-literal
      type aborts the expansion.
  entry .decodeOrigins   the source `(start, end)` of each literal part an `Origins` type encodes, inside a macro
      `Interpolation.decodeOrigins[origins]` returns the pairs in source order; an element it
      cannot read decodes as `(0, 0)`, the marker for an unknown origin.
  entry .literalOrigins   the source `(start, end)` of each string literal in a `StringContext` tree
      Walks the `StringContext.apply(…)` tree through `Inlined`, `Typed`, `Block` and `Apply`
      wrappers, collecting every string literal's position. If the number found differs from the
      expected count, every part is `(0, 0)`, so that errors land on the whole expression rather
      than at a wrong character.
  entry .sourcePosition   the source-file position of a parser offset into a literal's assembled text
      `Interpolation.sourcePosition(parts, origins, substitutionWidth, offset, length)` takes the
      offset an interpolator's parser reports into the "assembled" input — the parts joined with
      `substitutionWidth` characters standing in for each hole — and returns the `Position` in the
      source file of the character at fault, at least one character long, for `halt` to report
      against. Escapes which are longer in the source than in the value (`$$` and `\uHHHH`) are
      compensated for. An offset which falls in a hole, or in a part whose origin is unknown,
      gives the position of the whole expression.
  entry .buildMapping   maps each character index of a literal part's value to its index in the source text
      `Interpolation.buildMapping(source, value)` accounts for `$$` (two source characters for
      one `$`) and `\uHHHH` (six for one); an index below zero maps to `0` and one beyond the end
      to the last position.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val mapping = Interpolation.buildMapping("a$$b", "a$b")
      List(0, 1, 2, 3).map(mapping)   // List(0, 1, 3, 4)
      ```
  entry #Topic   the interpolator's target type, the type an `Interpolable` instance is sought for
  entry #Transport   the literal parts, as a tuple of string-literal types in reverse source order
  entry #Origins   each literal part's source `(start, end)`, as a tuple of integer-literal pairs in reverse order
  entry #apply   constructs the target value from the literal and its substituted values
      Called by an interpolated literal such as `slug"hello $name"`, with the substituted values
      as `insertions`. It needs a `Topic is Interpolable` in scope (normally in the target's
      companion) and returns whatever that instance's `interpolate` returns; being `transparent
      inline`, it keeps the precise type.
  entry #unapply   deconstructs a value against the literal, when the literal is used as a pattern
      Called by a pattern such as `case slug"hello" =>`. It needs a `Topic is Extrapolable` in
      scope, and returns the instance's `Extrapolation`.

entry .Interpolable   a typeclass building a value of its type from an interpolated literal's parts and holes
  detail
      `T is Interpolable` is what `Interpolation#apply` looks for. Its one method, `#interpolate`,
      is `inline`, and receives the literal's parts and origins as the type parameters `parts`
      and `origins` and the substituted values as `insertions`. A simple instance reads the parts
      with `compiletime.constValueTuple`; a checking one splices a macro which decodes them with
      `Interpolation.decodeParts`, parses them, and halts at `Interpolation.sourcePosition` on a
      fault, so a malformed literal never compiles.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A target type
        code
            import soundness.*

            case class Slug(text: Text)
      frame
        label An `Interpolable` instance in its companion: the parts arrive reversed
        code
            import soundness.*
            import scala.compiletime

            case class Slug(text: Text)

            object Slug:
              given interpolable: Slug is Interpolable:
                transparent inline def interpolate[parts <: Tuple, origins <: Tuple]
                  ( inline insertions: Any* )
                :   Slug =
                  Slug(compiletime.constValueTuple[parts].toList.reverse.mkString.tt)
      frame
        label A prefix method, and the interpolator is ready
        code
            import soundness.*
            import scala.compiletime

            case class Slug(text: Text)

            object Slug:
              given interpolable: Slug is Interpolable:
                transparent inline def interpolate[parts <: Tuple, origins <: Tuple]
                  ( inline insertions: Any* )
                :   Slug =
                  Slug(compiletime.constValueTuple[parts].toList.reverse.mkString.tt)

            extension (inline context: StringContext)
              transparent inline def slug: Interpolation = interpolation[Slug](context)

            slug"hello"   // Slug(t"hello")
      ##
      ```

      A production interpolator puts the parsing in a macro, so the same parser serves the
      literal at compile time and dynamic text at runtime:

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      inline given interpolable: MediaType is Interpolable:
        transparent inline def interpolate[parts <: Tuple, origins <: Tuple](inline insertions: Any*)
        :   MediaType =
          ${internal.mediaInterpolator[parts]('insertions)}
      ```
  refer contextual.Interpolation
  refer contextual.Insertion
  entry #Self   the interpolator's target type
  entry #interpolate   builds the target value from the literal's parts (type-level, reversed) and its substituted values
      `parts` is a tuple of string-literal types holding the literal's parts in reverse source
      order, `origins` their source positions likewise, and `insertions` the substituted values,
      in source order. Being `inline`, with `insertions` passed `inline`, a macro behind it sees
      each substituted expression and its type, and may resolve an `Insertion` or `Substitution`
      for each.

entry .Extrapolable   a typeclass matching a value of its type against an interpolated literal, as a pattern
  detail
      `T is Extrapolable` is what `Interpolation#unapply` looks for, so an interpolated literal
      can be a `case` pattern; it is how `json"…"` and `html"…"` patterns work. One prefix method
      serves both construction and matching.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import scala.compiletime

      case class Slug(text: Text)

      object Slug:
        given extrapolable: Slug is Extrapolable:
          transparent inline def extrapolate[parts <: Tuple, origins <: Tuple](scrutinee: Slug)
          :   Extrapolation[Slug] =
            scrutinee.text == compiletime.constValueTuple[parts].toList.reverse.mkString.tt

      extension (inline context: StringContext)
        transparent inline def slug: Interpolation = interpolation[Slug](context)

      Slug(t"hello") match
        case slug"hello" => t"matched"
        case _           => t"unmatched"
      ```
  refer contextual.Extrapolation
  refer contextual.Interpolable
  entry #Self   the type a pattern deconstructs
  entry #extrapolate   matches a scrutinee against the literal's parts, giving an `Extrapolation`
      Receives the parts and origins as type parameters, exactly as `Interpolable#interpolate`
      does, and the value being matched. It returns a `Boolean` for a pattern with no holes, and
      an `Option` of the bound value (or of a tuple of them) for one with holes.

entry .Extrapolation   what an `Extrapolable` match returns: a `Boolean`, or an `Option` of the bound values
    `Extrapolation[result]` is `Boolean | Option[Tuple | result]`, the three shapes Scala's
    pattern matching accepts from an `unapply`: a `Boolean` for a pattern which binds nothing, an
    `Option[result]` for one hole, and an `Option` of a tuple for several.

entry .Insertion   a typeclass: how a substituted value of one type enters an interpolator's input type
  detail
      `Insertion[input, value]` turns a `value` substituted into a hole into the interpolator's
      own `input` type, so the interpolator, not `toString`, decides how foreign values enter.
      A type with no instance cannot be substituted at all, which is how an interpolator forbids
      what it cannot make safe; a new type becomes substitutable with one instance, without
      touching the interpolator.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      given Insertion[Sh.Parameters, Text] = value => Sh.Parameters(value)
      given Insertion[Sh.Parameters, List[Text]] = values => Sh.Parameters(values*)
      ```

      `value` is contravariant, so an instance for a supertype serves its subtypes. A
      `Substitution` is an insertion which also tells the parser what the hole stands for.
  refer contextual.Substitution
  refer contextual.Embeddable
  entry .embeddable   a `Substitution` for any `Embeddable` type, labelled `"x"`, in implicit scope
      For a `value` which is `Embeddable`, this gives `Substitution[value.Operand, value, "x"]`,
      embedding through `Embeddable#embed`, so an interpolator which asks for substitutions finds
      one for every embeddable type without an instance of its own.
  entry #embed   converts a substituted value into the interpolator's input type

entry .Substitution   an `Insertion` which also gives the parser a label standing in for the hole
    `Substitution[input, value, label]` behaves at runtime exactly as `Insertion[input, value]`
    does. At compile time the singleton `Label` tells the interpolator's parser what the hole
    contained, so the parser can proceed as though a representative token of the substituted
    type were written there — `"0"` for a number, `""""` for a string — and check the *shape*
    of a literal with holes rather than give up at the first one. Where different holes accept
    different things (a URL's port takes a number, its path text), the label is what
    distinguishes them.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    enum SqlInput:
      case IntInput(value: Int)
      case StrInput(value: Text)

    given Substitution[SqlInput, Int, "0"] = SqlInput.IntInput(_)
    given Substitution[SqlInput, Text, "\"\""] = SqlInput.StrInput(_)
    ```

entry .Embeddable   a typeclass embedding a value as the `Operand` of an interpolator whose target is `Form`
  detail
      `T is Embeddable by Operand in Form` says that a `T` may be substituted into an
      interpolator targeting `Form`, entering as an `Operand`. It is the typeclass-style form of
      `Insertion`: one instance in the substituted type's companion, and `Insertion.embeddable`
      derives the corresponding `Substitution` from it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Slug(text: Text)
      case class Tag(name: Text)

      object Tag:
        given embeddable: Tag is Embeddable by Text in Slug = _.name

      Tag.embeddable.embed(Tag(t"widget"))   // t"widget"
      summon[Substitution[Text, Tag, "x"]].embed(Tag(t"widget"))   // t"widget"
      ```
  refer contextual.Insertion
  entry #embed   converts a value into the interpolator's operand type
  entry #contramap   an embedding for another type, through a function into this one
      `embeddable.contramap[Int](count => Tag(count.show))` is an `Int is Embeddable by Text in
      Slug`, which embeds an `Int` by first making a `Tag` of it. The result captures both this
      instance and the function.
